arly Dependent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707105} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707014} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707050}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121020 :tutorial/content "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations: \n\n  I. Swapping two rows\n\n  II. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\n  III. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix $A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Taking the determinant, we have\n$$\n\\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right| = 1 \\cdot 4 - 2 \\cdot 3 = -2.\n$$\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5ae8e63-4d2c-5491-9908-96fe94243ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121021 :tutorial/content "Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A),$ the corresponding elementary column operations on determinants have the same properties as the elementary row ones.\n\n- Swapping two columns changes the sign of the determinant.\n\n- Multiplying a column by a number scales the determinant by that number.\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column does not change the determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "bac0b72b-b76a-5a06-9312-d2b7b8ac4247" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3005 :tutorial/title "Column Operations on Determinants"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121034 :tutorial/content "Consider a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ which is given in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_1}}+({\\color{red}-2}){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}} = \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHere, we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is a **linear combination** of the vectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_1}}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}$ with coefficients ${\\color{red}3}$ and ${\\color{red}-2}$ respectively. \n\nIn general, a linear combination of $n$ vectors $\\mathbf{v_1},\\mathbf{v_2},\\dots,\\mathbf{v_n}$ is the *weighted sum*\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1}\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_1}+{\\color{red}x_2}\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_2}+\\cdots + {\\color{red}x_n}\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_n} = \\mathbf{u},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{red}x_1},{\\color{red}x_2},\\dots,{\\color{red}x_n}\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_1},\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_2},\\dots,\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_n}$ with coefficients (or *weights*) ${\\color{red}x_1},{\\color{red}x_2},\\dots,{\\color{red}x_n},$ respectively.\n\nFor instance, \n\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1} + {\\color{red}0} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}\n$$\n\n\nis the linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_1},\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_2},\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_3}$ with coefficients ${\\color{red}2}, {\\color{red}0}, {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}},$ respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "229ae855-226b-5f95-b050-b98257747800" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3186 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
